WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.734
We are tracking

00:00:00.734 --> 00:00:01.768
post Tropical

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:02.869
Cyclone Lee

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:04.938
as it continues up the New England coast.

00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:06.539
This huge storm continues

00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:08.174
to bring 50 to 60 mile per hour

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:09.042
winds

00:00:09.042 --> 00:00:09.876
and heavy rain

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:10.377
and storm

00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:12.012
surges to the New England coast.

00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:13.079
And CNN's Gary

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:15.181
Tuchman got up close with the storm

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:18.218
wall on board a plane that is tracking it

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:22.522
The Gulfstream four

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.858
is typically a business jet,

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:27.727
but this one is reconfigured

00:00:33.099 --> 00:00:34.234
and its business

00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:36.436
is to help protect lives.

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:38.171
These are the hurricane hunters

00:00:38.171 --> 00:00:40.306
eight scientists, engineers, pilots.

00:00:40.607 --> 00:00:42.275
They work for the National Oceanic

00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:43.843
and Atmospheric Administration,

00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:45.345
known as Noah.

00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:47.113
Paul Flaherty is the flight director

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:49.049
and a flight meteorologist.

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:49.682
We want to make sure

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:50.984
we're collecting data

00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:52.585
in data, sparse areas

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:53.253
in which

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:54.888
there's currently no data available

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:56.322
and very little data available

00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:57.791
for the weather models.

00:00:57.791 --> 00:00:59.659
To use to make forecasts

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:00.460
for this mission.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:01.161
This aircraft

00:01:01.161 --> 00:01:02.295
flies at altitudes

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:04.831
between 41 and 45,000 feet.

00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:07.867
It travels around 500 miles per hour

00:01:08.368 --> 00:01:09.302
This is essentially

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:11.404
a flying weather station,

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:12.739
a weather station

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:13.873
that goes to the weather

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:16.109
for the next 8 hours.

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:16.376
The men

00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:17.644
and women of the Noah Corps

00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:19.212
will flying in this high altitude

00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:20.713
reconnaissance jet

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:22.615
above, below, around

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:24.717
and in front of Hurricane Lee.

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:26.252
I think for now will be fine.

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:27.687
But yeah,

00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:28.621
looks like

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:31.591
plenty of space to maneuver around.

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:33.126
After the jet leaves, Lakeland,

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:34.861
Florida, skies are clear

00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:35.829
at this high altitude.

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:36.963
You can clearly see

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:39.332
storm churned whitecaps in the ocean.

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:40.400
It doesn't take long, though,

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.302
for the sunshine to disappear.

00:01:42.302 --> 00:01:44.070
The flight gets turbulent as Hurricane

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.372
Lee lurks ominously below us.

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:48.508
All the while science is taking place.

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:51.578
The screen shows 34 locations where a to

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:54.380
notice a drop sand or sand

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:56.249
will be dropped out of the plane.

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:57.450
So this is the next drop.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.052
So it's going to be drops.

00:01:59.052 --> 00:02:00.854
Rebecca Keller, Noah Engineer,

00:02:00.854 --> 00:02:02.755
what is in the drops?

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:04.991
So the drop zone consists of a sensor

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:06.459
and we have a circuit board

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:08.761
inside as well as a battery.

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:12.165
And the sensor is picking up humidity,

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:13.466
air temperature,

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:15.835
pressure, wind direction and wind speed.

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:17.770
And about every 10 minutes,

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:21.741
another drop sound with a parachute

00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:22.642
that is deployed

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:24.978
is launched to the ground

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:28.081
along with the science.

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:30.250
The plane also has radar in its nose,

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.318
Doppler radar on its tail,

00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:33.920
and two pilots up front

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.922
flying with a deep sense of purpose.

00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:37.957
I joined the No Air Corps, as did

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:39.125
all of my counterparts.

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:40.793
Because we love to serve our country.

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:42.662
We care about

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:44.330
our the citizens

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.132
and so it's really rewarding

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:46.499
to know

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:48.902
that I am like right at the front lines

00:02:48.902 --> 00:02:50.637
and risking my life

00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:51.037
in order

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:52.338
to help the lives

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.641
of everyone else that are back home.

00:02:54.641 --> 00:02:56.676
The marathon flight is almost over.

00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:56.943
All right.

00:02:56.943 --> 00:03:00.547
So that is the last song that

00:03:00.547 --> 00:03:01.181
you

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:03.583
did.

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:05.652
And as the plane heads back to Florida

00:03:05.652 --> 00:03:06.853
out the window,

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:09.656
looking at a spectacular sunset.

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:10.089
Yeah.

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:11.024
So we're out of the storm

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:12.859
environment, obviously. Um,

00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:14.594
that'd be just

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:14.994
great weather

00:03:14.994 --> 00:03:16.863
keeping us on our way back to Florida

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:18.398
as the plane gets ready to land.

00:03:18.398 --> 00:03:19.866
Time for the hurricane hunters

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:21.301
to decompress

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:22.669
and get mentally ready

00:03:22.669 --> 00:03:24.604
for more eight hour trips to come.

00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:25.138
So, yeah,

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:26.639
we kind of live up here,

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:27.707
spend more time together

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:28.875
than we do at home.

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:29.342
Go home,

00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:31.244
sleep, eat and repeat,

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:33.179
you know, and get back up here.

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:35.148
Start collecting the data again.

00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:35.582
Anderson,

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:36.649
the aircraft we were on

00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:39.385
is not NOAA's only hurricane hunter.

00:03:39.786 --> 00:03:41.888
They also fly the P-3 Orion.

00:03:42.222 --> 00:03:43.323
It's a larger plane.

00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:44.791
It fits more people at flies,

00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:45.858
low altitudes.

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:47.860
It flies through hurricane eye walls.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:48.861
I've been on it before.

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:49.562
Needless to say,

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:51.965
the turbulence is intense.

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:52.599
Either way,

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:53.766
the important data gathered

00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:55.034
from both these planes

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:56.436
and both these missions

00:03:56.436 --> 00:03:56.736
that is

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:58.972
then embedded into computer models

00:03:58.972 --> 00:04:01.040
makes it much easier for meteorologists

00:04:01.174 --> 00:04:02.642
to give accurate forecasts

00:04:04.344 --> 00:04:06.179
What a cool assignment.

00:04:06.179 --> 00:04:07.847
One that I'm glad I didn't have to do.

00:04:07.847 --> 00:04:08.681
Yeah, I know.

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:09.716
I mean, now

00:04:09.716 --> 00:04:11.050
that everybody's back on the ground

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:11.918
and safe is like,

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:13.286
oh, that was an excellent idea

00:04:13.286 --> 00:04:15.154
to send Gary up in that plane.

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:16.522
Gary Tuchman, thanks so much.

